estion/math-academy-id "q-54488" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/39723169158aa3bf15f57244e3ac4c4a3ffb1c2b0e3fc41b6db712cfab8ddbd9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases.  Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-54488/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-54488"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339080/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "2dc46f4c-7311-51be-a7ec-48d06c7c434b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339080/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "8186550d-859c-5feb-b891-cd2519c7cb4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339080/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "78087df0-84a9-5dca-8f45-8065bf21cea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339080/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-339080/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-339080/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339080" :question/id #uuid "0ed23e01-3b8b-53d3-bf5f-2d1f34d8be1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339080" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({7}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/f2858b7d6adc07c0f2196bbe8170f17fa02aff19d494726c08f7c03a419afd53.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function approaches zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339080/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339080"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339407/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "dc93ab5a-2fc6-5638-8e00-85569d3ed330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339407/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c467857-1a0b-5c78-84d7-b6e7d4e72f00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339407/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-339407/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339407" :question/id #uuid "685bf91b-cfa0-5d3f-9ff7-84b24dff711f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339407" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/99c2329c99005af33f4b62d61547e18dcaa7f04afc931df57439bd1ca08ff35c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339407/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339407"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339411/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "8d57480f-e01f-5395-8319-9798c6e73a71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339411/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "3eecbe5d-5169-5ecb-8d66-cf684eafd2b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339411/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "132f82cc-a838-5394-a0bf-b4439a4a55cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339411/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-339411/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" "q-339411/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339411" :question/id #uuid "0009578d-6d2c-52a3-8af9-429c07a34403" :question/math-academy-id "q-339411" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3} & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x-3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{-3}\\cdot {e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{-3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={({e}^{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/6590b80777c49b3d018fa38ce79c7fb14103365f783dac18135b8d3637ee724d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe negative factor $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-339411/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339411"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339413/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "48aaff29-a61c-516a-bbe1-eee2c8c6fb1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-339413/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1b0323-a9a4-50a1-93aa-709f148ea69d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-339413/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-339413/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-339413" :question/id #uuid "6fa418ff-7c76-5ae9-aad5-d6dfb417acfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-339413" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{-2x}) \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{-2x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-2})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/3795890dac6f542c8aa75d61deb8a03192a41532270b74eafcd972e82ac6721e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=125\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-339413/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339413"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
